ricebtw
=======

(formerly archbtw -- renamed 2026-10-21, GitHub redirects the old name)

I use Arch & Void btw.
blue pill or green pill, i'll take both.
also apparently Nix, XBPS & Flatpak now, don't tell pacman.
one package manager identity crisis wasn't enough,
so now five package managers live in two houses & refuse to talk to each other.
systemd vs runit? i run both. the war is a spectator sport & i'm in the
stands with popcorn, wearing both jerseys.
"which init is better?" my adhd.
Hyprland got ghosted for MangoWC.
NixOS? Never heard of her.

=======

Machines
--------

Arch Linux    daily driver. Everything below (Home Manager, Nix, the Phase
              list, GPU notes, pacman rules) is about this install, and
              rice/ (outside void-linux/) is its desktop backup:
              MangoWC + DankMaterialShell (moved from caelestia-shell-mango
              on 2026-10-24, see rice/arch-linux/dms/). Hyprland is no longer used
              (replaced by MangoWC in Aug 2026); its configs are kept as
              legacy reference only.
Void Linux    experimenting. runit + xbps, no systemd, no Nix, no Home
              Manager. MangoWC + DankMaterialShell. Everything for it lives in
              rice/void-linux/ and is self-contained -- see the README there.
              Nothing in the Nix/pacman sections applies to Void.

Home Manager configuration for sicmundus, layered on top of a normal Arch
Linux install. This is NOT NixOS. Arch (pacman) still owns the kernel,
bootloader, firmware, drivers, systemd, networking, filesystems, DBus, and
PipeWire. Nix + Home Manager owns the user environment on top of it: CLI
tools, dev toolchains, shell config, dotfiles, and (eventually) the desktop
stack. (Hyprland was the original target; it has since been replaced by MangoWC,
which is not Nix-managed -- see rice/README.)

Layout
------

flake.nix              entry point, pins nixpkgs-unstable + home-manager + agenix
home.nix               top-level config, wires up the modules below
modules/packages.nix  catch-all for packages with no dedicated module yet;
                       empty by design, not a placeholder for missing work
modules/cli.nix       CLI utilities -- the original Phase 1 set (bat, eza, fd,
                       ripgrep, fzf, jq, btop, fastfetch, lazygit, gh, direnv,
                       atuin) plus 22 leaf tools swept in later (zellij, yazi,
                       zoxide, yt-dlp, ncdu, cloc, bc, socat, pv, tree-sitter,
                       cbonsai, asciiquarium, gum, tty-clock, ttyper, p7zip,
                       inotify-tools, w3m, lynx, mpc, rmpc, wev, wtype,
                       wmctrl) -- see the file for the exact current list,
                       this README won't be re-synced on every addition
modules/git.nix       programs.git -- user/editor/credential.helper, written
                       to ~/.config/git/config (XDG path, not ~/.gitconfig)
modules/shell.nix     zsh: history, completion, autosuggestions, syntax
                       highlighting, fzf-tab, Powerlevel10k, env vars
modules/aliases.nix   all shell aliases -- deduped from the old .zshrc +
                       ~/.aliases, plus a Nix/Home Manager workflow set
                       (hms/hmb/hmg/ngc/etc, see the file header comment)
modules/dev.nix       dev toolchains (go, rustup, nodejs, python3, pipx,
                       cmake, ninja, llvm, clang)
modules/secrets.nix   agenix wiring (flake input + CLI); no secrets defined yet
modules/editors.nix   Neovim binary only -- ~/.config/nvim is self-managed lazy.nvim
modules/gui.nix       GUI apps that actually work under Nix (see GPU note below)
secrets/secrets.nix   agenix recipient manifest, gitignored (has a real
                       pubkey, deliberately not pushed) -- recreate from
                       ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub if this repo is ever cloned fresh
backup/               the original stock `home-manager init` output, kept
                       for reference, not imported by anything
rice/void-linux/      plain backup COPY of the Void Linux (experimental) setup:
                       MangoWC + DankMaterialShell dotfiles, bashrc/aliases,
                       xbps package lists, the DMS repo files. Separate from the
                       Arch rice around it, see rice/void-linux/README
rice/arch-linux/       Arch desktop dotfiles live in config/, home/, boot/ here;
                       dms/ is the active DankMaterialShell + MangoWC setup
                       (settings, mango config, keybinds), see its README
rice/                 plain backup COPY of the Arch desktop dotfiles
                       (MangoWC now; hypr/ is legacy from the Hyprland days;
                       also kitty, rofi, swaync, etc), plus
                       the full ~/Pictures/Wallpapers pool -- not managed or
                       symlinked by Home Manager, see rice/README. The
                       Wallpapers folder (~3.7G) is fully committed and pushed
                       but excluded from this repo's local working tree via
                       `git sparse-checkout` (see Workflow below) so it
                       doesn't sit on disk twice; it's still on GitHub in full.

Status
------

Done:  Phase 0 (repo skeleton), Phase 1 (CLI), Phase 2 (git), Phase 3 (shell),
       Phase 4 (dev), Phase 5 (Neovim), Phase 6 (GUI apps, partial -- see GPU note)
Skipped: Phase 7 (fonts/themes/icons/cursor) -- investigated, deliberately
         not migrating. Icons are a git-managed collection in
         ~/.local/share/icons (built on the "illogical-impulse" Hyprland
         dotfiles project, judging by illogical-impulse.svg in there), GTK/
         color theming is actively driven by matugen's generated templates
         (kitty, quickshell, qt5ct/qt6ct, Hyprland colors.conf, cava,
         swayosd, neovim), and the one font actually in use (JetBrainsMono
         Nerd Font, in kitty) is hard-blocked from pacman removal by yazi
         anyway. Nothing here would gain from a Nix package -- it's already
         better managed outside of pacman and Nix both.
Skipped: Phase 8 [historical -- written while Hyprland was the compositor;
         it has since been replaced by MangoWC] (Hyprland/Waybar/Hyprlock/Hypridle/Hyprpaper/Rofi/
         notifications/clipboard/portal) -- investigated, deliberately not
         migrating. hyprland (the live compositor, required by hyprshade),
         hyprlock (security-critical -- a broken lock screen can fail open,
         not just annoy you), hypridle (confirmed actively running, tightly
         IPC-coupled to hyprland/hyprlock), and xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland
         (other apps like steam/obs-studio depend on it working) all stay
         pacman-only permanently -- no realistic upside (nixpkgs versions
         already match pacman's almost exactly) against a uniquely severe
         downside (a broken live desktop, not just a broken command).
         hyprpaper isn't even running -- mpvpaper is the real live-wallpaper
         mechanism, confirmed via WallpaperPicker.qml. waybar/rofi/swaync/
         cliphist were left alone too: same GTK/layer-shell GPU-rendering
         uncertainty as the Phase 6 Electron apps, and testing them for real
         would mean launching a competing instance against the live bar/
         notifications mid-session -- not a risk worth taking for apps that
         already work fine on pacman.
Done:  Phase 9 (final cleanup) -- 12 orphaned packages left over from the
       Neovim migration removed (tree-sitter-*, libvterm, msgpack-c, etc,
       plus tree-sitter itself once it became a second-order orphan), pacman
       db verified clean, nix store gc'd (1792 dead paths, 1005.6 MiB freed,
       all 9 generations and current activation verified intact afterward)
Done:  post-Phase-1 sweep -- 22 additional leaf CLI tools (see modules/cli.nix
       for the list) added after confirming via `pacman -Qi` that every one
       had zero pacman reverse-deps (Required By: None); all 22 pacman
       packages fully removed after the Nix build/switch was verified
Done:  post-Phase-2 cleanup -- dropped the unused `sendemail`/gmail block
       from git.nix (no evidence of `git send-email` use, and it put a real
       email in this public repo's history for no benefit) and removed the
       stale plain ~/.gitconfig on disk, which predated this migration and
       was silently shadowing the Nix-managed ~/.config/git/config for
       anything both files set. Backed up locally to ~/.config/hyprbackup
       (never pushed) before deleting, not left as dead weight in the repo.

GPU acceleration under Nix (read before touching Phase 7/8)
-------------------------------------------------------------

Nix's bundled Mesa can't reach Arch's real GPU driver stack out of the box:
it looks for drivers at /run/opengl-driver (a NixOS-only convention). Fixed
the first hop with a symlink (/run/opengl-driver -> /usr, made permanent via
/etc/tmpfiles.d/opengl-driver.conf) -- this is safe, additive, doesn't touch
actual drivers/Mesa, just tells Nix binaries where Arch's already-working
ones live.

That fixes simple cases, but Electron/Chromium apps (tested: Obsidian) hit a
second wall: Arch's dri_gbm.so needs libgallium-*.so, which lives one
directory up (/usr/lib, not /usr/lib/gbm). Nix's dynamic linker doesn't
consult /etc/ld.so.cache the way a native Arch process does, so it can't find
it. Tried LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/usr/lib as a blanket fix -- this broke worse: it
loaded Arch's glibc ahead of Nix's own and crashed with a GLIBC_PRIVATE
symbol mismatch. Do not try that again without scoping it far more narrowly.

Decision: firefox, brave, obsidian, mpv stay pacman-only permanently (same
tier as python/nodejs -- see below). flameshot was verified clean (no GL/EGL
errors, doesn't need heavy compositing) and is Nix-managed. The real fix for
GPU-heavy apps, if ever wanted, is nixGL (github.com/guibou/nixGL) -- wraps
specific launches with a curated, correctly-scoped library path instead of a
blanket one. Not set up. Expect to hit this again in Phase 7/8 for anything
using GTK/Wayland layer-shell rendering (Waybar, Rofi, Hyprlock) -- check
each one for GL/EGL errors before assuming it'll behave like flameshot did.

What stays on pacman permanently (not a "not done yet", a deliberate never):

  zsh          it's /bin/zsh, your actual login shell in /etc/passwd
  git          hard-required by paru, yay, git-filter-repo, zed
  python       hard-required by ~90 packages incl. reflector, kitty, meson
  nodejs/npm   hard-required by zed
  ninja        hard-required by meson
  gdb          hard-required by debugedit
  fzf          hard-required by ani-cli, downgrade, fzf-tab-git
  jq           hard-required by yq
  llvm/clang   only soft/optional reverse deps, low priority to touch

Real regression hit and fixed: Nix's python3 (added Phase 4) shadowed
/usr/bin/python3 on PATH and broke virt-manager -- its #!/usr/bin/env python3
shebang resolved to Nix's bare python3 instead of Arch's, which has
python-gobject installed and Nix's didn't, so `import gi` failed. Fixed by
removing python3 from dev.nix's global home.packages entirely (see comment
there). General lesson: any Arch tool using `env python3` (or similar
PATH-dependent shebangs) for system integration is at risk from a globally
-shadowing Nix interpreter of the same name -- gdb/nodejs could theoretically
have the same class of issue if something similar surfaces; check for
ModuleNotFoundError-style breakage in system tools after adding any Nix
language runtime, not just after adding libraries directly.

For all of these, the Nix-provided copy is installed and shadows the pacman
one on PATH for your own use; pacman's copy is never removed because
something else on the system depends on it existing.

  firefox, brave, obsidian, mpv   GPU acceleration doesn't work cleanly
                                   through Nix's Mesa (see note above) --
                                   these have NO Nix-provided copy at all,
                                   pacman's is the only one and stays that way

Everything else that got migrated (bat, eza, fd, ripgrep, btop, fastfetch,
lazygit, gh, direnv, atuin, rustup, the zsh plugin packages, plus the 22
leaf tools listed in modules/cli.nix and programs.git's ~/.config/git/config)
had its pacman package fully removed after verification, plus a second leaf
batch (ani-cli, gifski, irssi, weechat, znc, luarocks, nyx, pamixer, sparse,
stress-ng, yq, wl-clip-persist, tesseract).

Note on starship: zsh theming is Powerlevel10k (modules/shell.nix), not
starship -- an earlier revision of this README wrongly claimed starship had
been migrated, when in fact it wasn't installed anywhere at all. It later
turned out fish's config.fish (a separate, non-Home-Manager-managed shell
config) calls `starship init fish` unconditionally, so the binary being
missing was an actual live breakage, not a doc inaccuracy. Fixed by adding
starship to modules/cli.nix. zsh (via p10k) and fish (via starship) are
two independent prompts on two independent shells; don't conflate them.

Workflow
--------

Never edit ~/.zshrc, ~/.gitconfig, etc directly again -- they're symlinks
into the Nix store now. Edit the module, then:

  cd ~/.config/home-manager
  git add -A                     # flakes only see git-tracked files
  nix flake check --no-build     # catches eval errors before building
  nix build .#homeConfigurations.sicmundus.activationPackage --no-link --print-out-paths
  # inspect the printed store path's home-files/ if the change is delicate
  home-manager switch --flake .#sicmundus
  git commit -m "..."            # one commit per verified change

Rollback
--------

  home-manager generations               # list all generations
  /nix/store/.../activate                # re-activate an older one directly
  git log --oneline                      # source-level history
  git revert / git reset --hard <commit> # undo a config change at the source

Removing a pacman package is only ever done after the Nix replacement is
installed, built, switched to, and functionally verified -- never before.

Sparse-checkout (rice/arch-linux/home/Pictures/Wallpapers)
------------------------------------------------

This repo's git history and GitHub remote have the full wallpaper pool
committed (~3.7G, 2226 files), but the local working tree does not
materialize it -- `git sparse-checkout` (non-cone mode) excludes just that
one path via .git/info/sparse-checkout:

  /*
  !/rice/arch-linux/home/Pictures/Wallpapers/

This avoids a duplicate on-disk copy (the real one lives at
~/Pictures/Wallpapers, untouched by any of this). The files are still fully
tracked -- `git ls-files -v` shows them with the `S` (skip-worktree) flag,
and `git status` correctly reports a clean tree. Fast prompt tools that
bypass git's own status logic (Powerlevel10k's gitstatusd, specifically)
don't honor skip-worktree and will misreport these as modified -- that's a
known cosmetic mismatch, not a real repo problem. To get the files back
locally: `git sparse-checkout disable` (or `add` the specific path).

Also: any single `git push` covering this history must stay under GitHub's
2 GiB pack-size hard limit. The wallpaper commits were split into ~1.2G
batches for this reason (see git log around the "wallpaper pool batch"
commits) -- if more large binary content is ever added to rice/, batch it
the same way rather than committing it all at once.
